/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The 10–20 system places scalp electrodes at fixed fractions of the head.
   - Each electrode feeds one channel of a multichannel amplifier.
   - A montage is the chosen pairing of electrodes.
   - A seizure is a sudden, synchronous discharge of cortical neurons.
   - Interictal spikes occur between seizures and help locate the focus.
   - An evoked potential (EP) is a small, time-locked brain response to a stimulus.
   - The EP is buried in ongoing EEG.
   - SNR1 is the signal-to-noise ratio in one trial (dimensionless); N is the number of averaged trials (no unit); SNRN is the ratio after averaging.
   - Magnetoencephalography (MEG) measures the magnetic field produced by the same cortical currents that make the EEG.
   - MEG is not distorted by the skull and scalp, so it localises sources well; temporal resolution matches EEG.
*/
