/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The chain starts at the patient.
   - Vin is the electrode signal in µV; G is the amplifier gain (no unit); Vout is the amplifier output; Venv is the envelope, the output of a low-pass smoother with a time constant of about 0.1–1 s; T is the threshold, in the same unit as Venv 
   - EEG alpha training uses scalp electrodes, often over the occipital region.
   - EMG relaxation training uses surface electrodes over a muscle such as the frontalis or forearm.
   - Threshold choice decides the training.
   - Mains interference is a common fault.
   - A comparator can chatter when the signal sits near threshold.
   - Safety is still needed because electrodes touch the patient.
*/
