/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A pedobarograph measures pressure between the foot and the floor.
   - P is pressure in pascal (Pa); F is the force on one sensor cell in newton (N); A is that cell's area in square metre (m²). 1 kPa = 1000 Pa.
   - Calibration uses known loads applied over a known area.
   - Clinics use peak pressure to find hot spots under a diabetic foot and contact area to check load sharing.
   - One gait cycle starts when a foot touches the floor and ends when the same foot touches again.
   - Step length is the distance between successive contacts of opposite feet.
   - v is walking speed in m/s; Lstep is step length in m; cstep is cadence in steps/s (cadence in steps/min divided by 60).
   - Speed also equals stride length times stride frequency.
   - A goniometer measures joint angle.
   - An accelerometer measures acceleration.
*/
